# The traffic-analysis tier. Load it with `SURICATA_TRAFFIC=1`.
#
# These rules never mention a WireGuard byte, a port or an address. They match
# a flow's *shape* — how much, how long, in which direction, how random — which
# is what a middlebox that is willing to profile rather than fingerprint has to
# work with. That is why they catch awg, whose whole point is to hide the
# protocol, and why they are also crude: a busy, encrypted, bidirectional UDP
# flow is what a tunnel looks like and what several other things look like too.
#
# The numbers behind a flag (packet sizes, direction volumes, inter-arrival
# times, entropy) are printed by `traffic_features.py`, which is the real
# substance of this tier; the rules are the alerting face of it. Reported as a
# `traffic=` column and, like `hard`, never fails the WireGuard verdict.

# A sustained, high-volume, encrypted UDP flow: enough packets to be a tunnel
# rather than a probe, enough bytes to be carrying data, long enough to have
# handshaked and settled, and random enough to be encrypted.
alert udp any any -> any any (msg:"Traffic: sustained encrypted UDP flow"; flow.pkts:toserver,>30; flow.bytes:toserver,>20000; flow.age:>10; entropy: value > 6.0; sid:9930001; rev:1;)

# Substantial volume in both directions: most client UDP is request/response
# and lopsided; a tunnel carries the peer's traffic back.
alert udp any any -> any any (msg:"Traffic: bidirectional UDP volume"; flow.bytes:toserver,>10000; flow.bytes:toclient,>10000; sid:9930002; rev:1;)

# A long-lived, many-packet UDP flow: the shape of a persistent tunnel, and the
# place a keepalive cadence lives.
alert udp any any -> any any (msg:"Traffic: long-lived many-packet UDP flow"; flow.age:>20; flow.pkts:toserver,>50; sid:9930003; rev:1;)
